Based on your earlier setup (Node.js 18/Express + DeepL API v2, with Redis caching), here’s a lean, low-latency pattern to implement translation API calls efficiently and reliably.

Key tactics for speed and stability
- Reuse clients and connections: create one DeepL client/axios instance with HTTP keep-alive; don’t instantiate per request.
- Cache first, then call: Redis (cross-instance) + small in-process LRU. Key by (source|auto, target, text, options).
- Deduplicate in-flight calls: if two identical requests arrive, share the same promise instead of calling the provider twice.
- Batch when possible: send arrays of texts in one API call (DeepL and Google both support batching).
- Concurrency control: cap concurrent outgoing calls to avoid queuing/429 slowdowns (e.g., a small p-limit).
- Smart timeouts + retries: short timeouts; exponential backoff for 429/5xx with jitter; fail fast on non-retryable errors.
- Specify source language when you can: skip extra detection overhead if you already know it.
- Place servers close to the provider: choose regions near DeepL/Google endpoints; keep network paths short.
- Instrument p50/p95 and cache hit rate: measure end-to-end latency and provider time to spot bottlenecks.

Compact Node.js example (DeepL v2, fast path)
- Features: Redis cache, keep-alive HTTP agent, in-flight dedup, batching, retries with backoff.

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const https = require('https');
const Redis = require('ioredis');

const DEEPL_KEY = process.env.DEEPL_API_KEY;
const redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL || 'redis://localhost:6379');

const agent = new https.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 64 });
const deepl = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://api.deepl.com/v2',
  timeout: 4000,
  httpsAgent: agent,
  headers: { Authorization: `DeepL-Auth-Key ${DEEPL_KEY}` }
});

const inflight = new Map(); // cache of promises for dedup
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));

function cacheKey(texts, targetLang, sourceLang, options = {}) {
  const t = Array.isArray(texts) ? texts.join('\u0001') : texts;
  return `tr:${sourceLang || 'auto'}:${targetLang}:${options.preserve || 0}:${t}`;
}

async function translateFast({ texts, targetLang, sourceLang, options = {} }) {
  const key = cacheKey(texts, targetLang, sourceLang, options);
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  if (inflight.has(key)) return inflight.get(key); // share the in-flight call

  const call = (async () => {
    // retry with exponential backoff for retryable errors
    const maxRetries = 3;
    let attempt = 0;
    let lastErr;
    while (attempt <= maxRetries) {
      try {
        const body = {
          text: Array.isArray(texts) ? texts : [texts],
          target_lang: targetLang,
          ...(sourceLang ? { source_lang: sourceLang } : {}),
          ...(options.preserve ? { preserve_formatting: true } : {})
        };
        const res = await deepl.post('/translate', body);
        const out = res.data.translations.map(t => t.text);
        const result = Array.isArray(texts) ? out : out[0];

        // cache for 1 hour (tune TTL to your freshness needs)
        await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(result), 'EX', 3600);
        return result;
      } catch (err) {
        lastErr = err;
        const status = err.response?.status;
        const retryable = status === 429 || (status >= 500 && status < 600) || err.code === 'ECONNRESET' || err.code === 'ETIMEDOUT';
        if (!retryable || attempt === maxRetries) break;
        const backoff = 200 * Math.pow(2, attempt) + Math.floor(Math.random() * 100);
        await sleep(backoff);
        attempt++;
      }
    }
    throw lastErr;
  })();

  inflight.set(key, call);
  try {
    return await call;
  } finally {
    inflight.delete(key);
  }
}

// Example usage:
// Single text
// const result = await translateFast({ texts: 'Hello!', targetLang: 'ES', sourceLang: 'EN' });
// Batch
// const results = await translateFast({ texts: ['Hello', 'Good morning'], targetLang: 'DE' });
```

If you prefer Google Translate (v3)
- Use the official @google-cloud/translate client (it uses HTTP/2 and connection reuse).
- The same caching/dedup/backoff patterns apply.
- Google supports passing an array of contents to translateText for batching.

Additional practical tips
- Do not overuse compression for short texts; it adds CPU overhead without much benefit. For large payloads, enable gzip.
- Normalize inputs before keying cache (trim, collapse whitespace) to increase hit rates without changing meaning.
- If your UI sends bursts of identical requests (e.g., typing), debounce at the client and/or coalesce at the server.
- Keep a small in-process LRU cache (e.g., 500 items) in front of Redis to avoid a round-trip for hot keys.
- Monitor: log provider RTT separately from total request time; watch p95+, error rate, 429s, and cache hit rate per route.

